anticipated. Explain roles, options and

210
00:19:45,599 --> 00:19:50,879
next steps in plain language. Check what
is workable for the family now. A plan

211
00:19:50,880 --> 00:19:55,687
that looks simple within a school
timetable may interact with demands we

212
00:19:55,688 --> 00:20:01,172
don't know about. Avoid judging the pace
of a response, the choices someone makes,

213
00:20:01,173 --> 00:20:06,386
or the time they need to consider an
option. This brings our environment-first

214
00:20:06,387 --> 00:20:11,600
thinking into the meeting itself. Where
might the school's language, timing or

215
00:20:11,601 --> 00:20:16,949
process create a barrier? What could we
change? A different format, questions in

216
00:20:16,950 --> 00:20:21,960
advance, a quieter place, or clearer
follow-up may change the experience of

217
00:20:21,961 --> 00:20:27,377
consultation. Keep asking what you need to
understand. Listening and clarity give

218
00:20:27,378 --> 00:20:30,763
us a stronger basis for solving something
together.

219
00:20:32,280 --> 00:20:37,023
On page twenty-five you'll find the
Consultation checklist. You can use it

220
00:20:37,024 --> 00:20:41,702
before a meeting, and return to it
afterwards. Start with a clear, shared

221
00:20:41,703 --> 00:20:46,836
purpose. What are we trying to understand
or decide together? Use open questions

222
00:20:46,837 --> 00:20:51,775
that allow an answer you haven't already
chosen. Provide more than one way to

223
00:20:51,776 --> 00:20:57,104
contribute, and respect the knowledge the
student and family bring. Record what you

224
00:20:57,105 --> 00:21:01,588
hear accurately. It can help to check your
understanding aloud: have I

225
00:21:01,589 --> 00:21:06,657
understood that correctly? Then agree an
action, who is responsible and how you

226
00:21:06,658 --> 00:21:11,011
will follow up. If something remains
undecided, make that clear too.

227
00:21:11,012 --> 00:21:15,755
Afterwards, ask yourself whether the
consultation changed or extended your

228
00:21:15,756 --> 00:21:20,824
understanding. Could everyone contribute
in a workable way? Do people know what

229
00:21:20,825 --> 00:21:26,023
happens next? Ask, listen and follow up. A
checklist or planning tool can help us

230
00:21:26,024 --> 00:21:31,157
remember those steps, while the quality of
the consultation still depends on our

231
00:21:31,158 --> 00:21:33,301
attention to the people involved.

232
00:21:34,840 --> 00:21:40,095
Let's look first at consulting students.
Begin by presuming competence, and make

233
00:21:40,096 --> 00:21:44,885
communication accessible. That might
involve augmentative and alternative

234
00:21:44,886 --> 00:21:50,075
communication, visuals, writing, drawing,
video or symbols. Offer questions and

235
00:21:50,076 --> 00:21:54,732
meeting information beforehand, simple
choices where useful, and enough

236
00:21:54,733 --> 00:21:59,389
processing time. Notice the sensory
environment. Build on the student's

237
00:21:59,390 --> 00:22:04,645
strengths and interests. Behaviour may
communicate preferences or difficulty, so

238
00:22:04,646 --> 00:22:09,502
observe carefully and check your
interpretation wherever possible. Trusted

239
00:22:09,503 --> 00:22:14,692
supporters can assist communication while
keeping the student's own perspective

240
00:22:14,693 --> 00:22:19,815
central. What a student tells us needs to
influence what we do. With families,

241
00:22:19,816 --> 00:22:24,871
begin with the student's strengths,
interests and what works. Listen to their

242
00:22:24,872 --> 00:22:29,528
knowledge without assuming you already
know their experience. Use plain

243
00:22:29,529 --> 00:22:34,053
language, explain school terms and respect
differences in culture and

244
00:22:34,054 --> 00:22:38,510
perspective. Some parents may be
neurodivergent themselves, so check

245
00:22:38,511 --> 00:22:43,434
communication preferences rather than
making assumptions. Use real examples

246
00:22:43,435 --> 00:22:48,424
from school to discuss needs, goals,
participation and possible adjustments.

247
00:22:48,425 --> 00:22:53,547
Check shared understanding, agree next
steps and keep the conversation open as

248
00:22:53,548 --> 00:22:58,337
circumstances change. This extends the
consultation work we introduced in

249
00:22:58,338 --> 00:23:03,460
Session Four. Choose one practice from
each column that you could try with the

250
00:23:03,461 --> 00:23:07,718
student and family you have in mind. Pause
to note those choices.

251
00:23:15,720 --> 00:23:20,181
As you look at your two choices, check
what they would make easier for the

252
00:23:20,182 --> 00:23:24,399
person involved. Can the student
communicate more comfortably? Can the

253
00:23:24,400 --> 00:23:29,166
family prepare, ask a question or add
knowledge that might otherwise be missed?

254
00:23:29,167 --> 00:23:34,056
Keep those choices with your conversation
notes. Consultation becomes more useful

255
00:23:34,057 --> 00:23:38,335
when we adapt it to the people involved,
and then act on what we learn.

256
00:23:39,841 --> 00:23:44,723
Asking is part of the job. Early in your
career, it can feel as though you should

257
00:23:44,724 --> 00:23:49,423
already know, but a well-placed question
can help a student get useful support

258
00:23:49,424 --> 00:23:54,307
sooner. Ask families what works at home.
Ask the student what helps and what they

259
00:23:54,308 --> 00:23:58,702
would like you to understand. Ask
colleagues too. Teacher aides, learning

260
00:23:58,703 --> 00:24:03,646
support staff and others who have known a
student over time can hold knowledge you

261
00:24:03,647 --> 00:24:08,285
haven’t had the opportunity to gather yet.
For relief or casual teachers, the

262
00:24:08,286 --> 00:24:13,107
classroom teacher, support staff in the
room and the student can all be starting

263
00:24:13,108 --> 00:24:17,624
points. Share and seek information through
the school’s usual processes. It

264
00:24:17,625 --> 00:24:22,507
is fine to say that you are still finding
something out. You can explain what you

265
00:24:22,508 --> 00:24:27,024
know, what you need to check and who you
will ask. That is more useful than

266
00:24:27,025 --> 00:24:31,786
guessing. Look back at the asking list
from Session Four. Who is one person you

267
00:24:31,787 --> 00:24:36,303
could ask about a student in the next
week? Note the person or role and the

268
00:24:36,304 --> 00:24:40,026
question you will take to them. Pause here
if you need longer.

269
00:24:48,041 --> 00:24:53,356
Keep that person and question with your
plan. Asking becomes a useful habit when

270
00:24:53,357 --> 00:24:58,807
it leads to listening, a clear next step
and follow-up. The people on your support

271
00:24:58,808 --> 00:25:02,575
map are there to help you think and act
within your role.

272
00:25:04,081 --> 00:25:08,889
Partnership becomes particularly important
when a student's capacity runs

273
00:25:08,890 --> 00:25:13,565
out. The terms on this slide are meltdown,
shutdown, and what the slide

274
00:25:13,566 --> 00:25:18,908
calls School Can't. That is, the student
cannot attend school. We are considering

275
00:25:18,909 --> 00:25:23,918
these through capacity, accumulated
demands and the parts of experience that

276
00:25:23,919 --> 00:25:28,994
may be difficult for others to see. These
experiences need a response without

277
00:25:28,995 --> 00:25:33,269
blame. Describing them as wilful
misbehaviour, or as a failure of

278
00:25:33,270 --> 00:25:37,744
parenting, can close down the
understanding we need. Home and school

279
00:25:37,745 --> 00:25:42,486
may see different parts of the student's
day. A difference between those

280
00:25:42,487 --> 00:25:47,763
observations is a reason to listen and
learn more. Bring back the questions from

281
00:25:47,764 --> 00:25:52,906
Sessions Two and Three. What has the day
been asking of this student? What may

282
00:25:52,907 --> 00:25:58,049
have accumulated? What information does
the student have, and what might their

283
00:25:58,050 --> 00:26:02,725
family know about recovery? Keep the focus
on safety, what preceded the

284
00:26:02,726 --> 00:26:07,935
situation, what supports recovery, and
what can change in the environment, task

285
00:26:07,936 --> 00:26:12,744
or response. Follow your school’s
wellbeing, safety and support processes

286
00:26:12,745 --> 00:26:17,553
and involve the appropriate staff. Return
to the contacts and pathway you

287
00:26:17,554 --> 00:26:22,295
identified in Session Three. A new teacher
does not need to resolve this

288
00:26:22,296 --> 00:26:27,372
alone. The partnership task here is to
share information respectfully, listen

289
00:26:27,373 --> 00:26:32,648
carefully and identify changes that may
reduce demands and improve the student's

290
00:26:32,649 --> 00:26:33,382
experience.

291
00:26:34,921 --> 00:26:39,821
Here are two Positive Partnerships tools
that can help make those conversations

292
00:26:39,822 --> 00:26:44,533
concrete. On the left, the Working
Together Communication Tool asks how home

293
00:26:44,534 --> 00:26:49,433
and school will communicate, how often,
about what, and what the next steps and

294
00:26:49,434 --> 00:26:54,145
dates will be. You can discuss options
such as text, email, an app, phone, a

295
00:26:54,146 --> 00:26:58,732
communication book or face-to-face
conversation. Choose what is useful and

296
00:26:58,733 --> 00:27:03,444
workable together. On the right, the
Partnership Planning Template asks what

297
00:27:03,445 --> 00:27:08,407
has worked, what has been challenging and
what could help. It gives examples and

298
00:27:08,408 --> 00:27:13,057
space for your own ideas. You might use it
to explore meeting arrangements,

299
00:27:13,058 --> 00:27:18,209
feeling heard, communication or including
the young person's voice. For example, if

300
00:27:18,210 --> 00:27:22,858
a family says phone calls are hard to
manage during work, you could use the

301
00:27:22,859 --> 00:27:27,696
tools to discuss another option and agree
when it will be reviewed. That is an

302
00:27:27,697 --> 00:27:32,408
illustrative use of the prompts; the
family may suggest something different.

303
00:27:32,409 --> 00:27:36,680
Ask your mentor or leader which
partnership tools your school already

304
00:27:36,681 --> 00:27:41,581
uses. Use the tools with people. Leave
room for their priorities to change what

305
00:27:41,582 --> 00:27:46,481
gets written. A completed page is useful
when it records a shared understanding

306
00:27:46,482 --> 00:27:48,805
that can guide the next conversation.

307
00:27:50,321 --> 00:27:55,151
The NCCD Meetings Toolkit is another
practical resource. NCCD stands for the

308
00:27:55,152 --> 00:28:00,240
Nationally Consistent Collection of Data
on School Students with Disability. You

309
00:28:00,241 --> 00:28:05,457
can find the toolkit on the official NCCD
website. Its sections support requesting

310
00:28:05,458 --> 00:28:10,353
and managing meetings, being prepared,
managing emotions, keeping records and

311
00:28:10,354 --> 00:28:15,119
considering whether a meeting was
successful. Use it to make the purpose of

312
00:28:15,120 --> 00:28:19,950
a meeting, the consultation process,
possible adjustments, evidence and next

313
00:28:19,951 --> 00:28:25,103
steps clear. Explain responsibilities and
follow-up in a way that the student and

314
00:28:25,104 --> 00:28:29,999
family can understand. People need to know
why a meeting is happening, who is

315
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:35,216
involved and how they can contribute. You
may contribute to these meetings without

316
00:28:35,217 --> 00:28:40,176
leading them. Find out your role, who
makes the relevant decisions and who can

317
00:28:40,177 --> 00:28:45,072
help with your questions. Follow the
processes used in your school or system,

318
00:28:45,073 --> 00:28:50,032
and use current official NCCD information
when you need specific guidance. The

319
00:28:50,033 --> 00:28:54,798
toolkit gives you a way to prepare and
review the process while keeping the

320
00:28:54,799 --> 00:28:57,890
discussion connected to the student's
experience.

321
00:28:59,400 --> 00:29:04,864
There are also practical support pathways
beyond today's workshop. Page thirty of

322
00:29:04,865 --> 00:29:09,919
your Participant Guide brings the
resources together. Positive Partnerships

323
00:29:09,920 --> 00:29:15,178
provides resources and learning options
for educators and families. This slide

324
00:29:15,179 --> 00:29:20,575
also shows Autism Connect, the national
autism information helpline, with phone,

325
00:29:20,576 --> 00:29:25,834
email and webchat options. Disability
Gateway can help people with disability,

326
00:29:25,835 --> 00:29:31,299
families and carers find information and
services. Carer Gateway provides support

327
00:29:31,300 --> 00:29:36,627
for carers. The contact details are on the
slide, so pause if you would like to

328
00:29:36,628 --> 00:29:41,750
record them. Choose a pathway that fits
the person's question, and check its

329
00:29:41,751 --> 00:29:47,010
website for the available support. We can
make families aware of these options

330
00:29:47,011 --> 00:29:49,879
while listening to what they want and
need.

331
00:29:52,881 --> 00:29:58,058
Before we finish this session, let's
prepare the bridge to one purposeful

332
00:29:58,059 --> 00:30:03,667
conversation. Return to the student you
have in mind and your day-one setup and

333
00:30:03,668 --> 00:30:08,989
asking list. Open page twenty-five, Bridge
to the next family conversation.

334
00:30:08,990 --> 00:30:14,167
Work through these questions privately.
What needs to be understood? What

335
00:30:14,168 --> 00:30:19,704
question will open the conversation? How
will the student's voice be included?

336
00:30:19,705 --> 00:30:25,170
What can be decided together? How and when
will the school follow up? Use the

337
00:30:25,171 --> 00:30:30,563
opening question you wrote during The
first two minutes on page twenty-four,

338
00:30:30,564 --> 00:30:35,813
and note who could help you prepare. Bring
in the process change you chose

339
00:30:35,814 --> 00:30:41,279
during the power-dynamics activity. Make
it part of how the conversation will

340
00:30:41,280 --> 00:30:46,672
happen, as well as what you will ask. You
are preparing your own purpose and

341
00:30:46,673 --> 00:30:52,138
approach. Leave space for the student's
and family's knowledge to change your

342
00:30:52,139 --> 00:30:57,603
understanding and the next step. Keep
names out of your workshop notes. Allow

343
00:30:57,604 --> 00:31:03,141
about four minutes to write. Pause the
video now, and resume when you're ready

344
00:31:03,142 --> 00:31:04,721
to close this session.

345
00:31:12,761 --> 00:31:17,885
Take a final look at the question you plan
to open with. Does it invite the

346
00:31:17,886 --> 00:31:23,218
other person's knowledge? Have you left
something meaningful to understand and

347
00:31:23,219 --> 00:31:28,412
decide together? Keep these notes. In
Session Six, you'll use them to plan a

348
00:31:28,413 --> 00:31:33,538
real conversation on page twenty-eight,
alongside one classroom action. The

349
00:31:33,539 --> 00:31:39,009
people on your asking list can help you
prepare or take part where needed. We'll

350
00:31:39,010 --> 00:31:44,065
bring the day together through belonging,
capacity and joy, and choose the

351
00:31:44,066 --> 00:31:49,329
practical next steps you want to carry
back to school. Thank you for the care

352
00:31:49,330 --> 00:31:52,930
